findings.description:
Rows (c), (d), (e) and (f) list CF as owner of the architecture-from-code, the existing-state slice plan, conventions reconciliation and docs/CI mapping. But the principles say cf "orchestrates nothing", exposes only read-only primitives, and never parses prose. The agent writes these artifacts, driven by prompts in ai-project-guide. That repo is "a dependency, not part of this initiative", and its retroactive-marker rule is "still pending". So the load-bearing content is owned by a repo outside this initiative, and part of it isn't written.

What cf actually owns is:
- the detection report;
- prompt delivery;
- the checker and gate changes;
- the `setup-ide` resolution application.

The table should say this.

The "Concept" row has the same weakness. It says "exactly one writer per run" but doesn't say who chooses between squadron 363 and the guide prompt. The only enforcement is prompt wording.
